<header class="site-header">
  <p class="eyebrow">An open reference for understanding parametric font design</p>
  <h1>Parametric source design</h1>
  <p>
    Variable fonts make it possible to interpolate between many design states.
    Parametric source design asks a more specific question: which individual
    transformations make up those states, and how can each one be controlled
    clearly and consistently?
  </p>
</header>

<div class="intro-content">
  <section>
    <h2>Parameters, sources, and transformations</h2>
    <p>
      This documentation examines the design decisions behind parametric
      sources. It is intended for type designers who are familiar with
      interpolation and want to understand how a typeface can be organised
      around a set of smaller, purposeful controls.
    </p>
    <p>
      A parametric axis controls one limited transformation in a typeface. It
      might change the thickness of a particular kind of stroke, the width of a
      character, the position of an alignment, or the size of a serif.
    </p>
    <p>
      A parametric source functions like a master for one of these
      transformations. Rather than describing a complete named style, it
      records how a specific part of the design should change. Together, the
      sources form a system from which more familiar design states can be
      constructed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From canonical axes to design axes</h2>
    <p>
      Canonical axes describe broad typographic dimensions such as horizontal
      and vertical proportions, stroke thickness, spacing, and serif shape.
      They provide a shared way to name the kinds of transformation that may be
      present in a typeface.
    </p>
    <aside class="note">
      <p class="note-label">Example</p>
      <p>
        A canonical vertical-proportion axis describes a broad typographic
        dimension. In a particular typeface, however, uppercase, lowercase, and
        figures may need to change independently. The project therefore divides
        that broad dimension into separate parameters, each designed for the
        behaviour of a specific group of glyphs.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What this documentation records</h2>
    <p>
      This is an open research archive. It records the intended behaviour of
      individual parameters and the evidence used to evaluate them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The transformations each parameter is expected to control.</li>
      <li>The glyph groups and constructions that reveal those transformations.</li>
      <li>Related parameters that may need to change together.</li>
      <li>Design-specific exceptions and unresolved questions.</li>
      <li>Sources, measurements, mappings, and proof specimens.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The documentation does not treat a parameter as a fixed recipe. It
      provides a way to inspect the reasoning behind a system, compare
      implementations, and identify where a rule holds, where it needs
      refinement, and why.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to use this archive</h2>
     <p>
    This archive is organised using the Diátaxis documentation framework. It
    separates four different kinds of help, so that learning a process,
    completing a task, understanding an idea, and looking up specific
    information do not become mixed together.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tutorials</strong> provide guided learning through a complete example.</li>
    <li><strong>How-to guides</strong> help with a specific practical task.</li>
    <li><strong>Explanation</strong> examines the concepts and reasoning behind parametric sources.</li>
    <li><strong>Reference</strong> provides factual material for close inspection, including parameters, source data, mappings, and proof specimens.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Begin with the explanation of canonical axes, design axes, and parametric
    sources. Then follow a project or parameter into the reference material as
    needed.
  </p>
  </section>

